<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ordoño I of Asturias</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 821–<a href="/years/866">866</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ordoño I was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> from 850 to 866, the son and successor of Ramiro I. He resettled and fortified frontier towns such as León, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astorga">Astorga</a> and Tui, pushing Christian settlement south of the Cantabrian mountains. In 859 he defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musa-ibn-musa">Musa ibn Musa</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-qasi">Banu Qasi</a> at Albelda, and his reign also saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids on the Galician coast driven off.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ordono-i-of-asturias.jpg" alt="Ordoño I of Asturias" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ordono1of_Asturias.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ordoño was born about 821 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a>, at the court of Alfonso II, and was probably raised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucus-augusti">Lugo</a>, the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, where his father Ramiro I had been made governor. He received his education and military training there. When Ramiro went to Bardulia to marry his second wife, Paterna, Ordoño was named governor of Galicia. During his father's absence Alfonso II died and the nobles elected Count Nepocian as king. Ordoño began to raise an army to help his father claim the throne, but could not leave his post, and his army went unused. When Ramiro prevailed he confirmed his son in his position.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After his father's death in 850 Ordoño became the first king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a> to ascend the throne without election. He first faced a rebellion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a>, supported by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-qasi">Banu Qasi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, and defeated a Moorish force near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> before it could attack Bardulia. He later fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musa-ibn-musa">Musa ibn Musa</a>, a Muslim Goth who controlled Zaragoza, Albelda, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tudela">Tudela</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-alfonso-iii">Chronicle of Alfonso III</a>, Ordoño divided his army, besieging Albelda with one half and engaging Musa's forces at Monte Laturce with the other. The battle was a massacre, in which Musa's brother-in-law Garcia and over 10,000 of his cavalry were said to have been killed, and Musa fled.</p><p>Musa's son Lupo is said to have submitted to Ordoño and remained his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> for life. Ordoño also defeated and imprisoned Moneror, king of Talamanca, and Zeth, king of Coria, sacked their villages and enslaved their inhabitants. He led the resettlement of Tui, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astorga">Astorga</a>, León and Amaya, which had been left deserted after Alfonso II conquered them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ordoño died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a> on 27 May 866 after suffering from gout and was buried in the Cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a>. He was described as a beloved king, and was succeeded by his son Alfonso III, who came to be called the first Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ordoño I of Asturias” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Musa ruled Tudela, Zaragoza and Huesca and was called the third king of Spain, until <b>Ordoño I of Asturias</b> defeated him at Albelda in 859.</blockquote><a href="/state/banu-qasi">Banu Qasi</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.36,-5.85&amp;year=850" aria-label="Oviedo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4409 625 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ordoño I of Asturias"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4859 895h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4859 895h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.36,-5.85&amp;year=850">What was here in 850 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
